$DIR/main.p:39:10: cannot pass &arr to g, which keeps it after returning
    &arr is a function literal that uses x. A function literal that uses
    variables of the function around it refers to those variables where they
    live, in f's frame (x is declared at main.p:36). g passes a pointer loaded
    from p[i] to call (main.p:32), which passes s to s.String (which may call
    main.(HF).String) (main.p:11), which stores h in package variable keepF
    (main.p:27), so &arr would outlive x.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change g to take a value or an owner, which it can keep: an owned[T] from
    new, a shared[T], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
